tests:
  unit: { count: 278, covers: "Both halves of the epic at the level where each rule lives. THE CORE (W1): the sender's header rules row by row - Content-Length is the body's, Host follows the URL unless the caller wrote one and never carries credentials, the connection headers are dropped, several Cookie headers join as the proxy does, order and duplicates kept - the failure causes in words (refused, unresolvable, TLS rejected, no reply head within 30 s), a finished send marked sent and linked to its seed, a stalled reply body landing marked unread; the doors' override merge (an absent field is the seed's own value, a given header list replaces the seed's whole), the uncaptured-seed-body refusal naming the absence, the credentials-in-URL refusal, and the sent source token round-tripping through both projections and ?source=. THE APP (W3): draft.ts's rules without React - seed to draft, every row mark, the removed count, the three body forms and the binary stand-in, the request each edit produces (only what differs reaches the replay door), the URL gate - and the core-owned header list pinned to src/sender.rs by reading its source; useDraft's one-draft door rule and in-flight window; the composer's states (send disabled exactly while sending..., a failure with no pill, focus returning to send, row add/remove/revert focus); Field's multi-line, verbatim and read-only forms under one box and no judged state; the sent mark, the replay control and the new-request entry. THE GUARD (W4): the e2e run tally's judge and the static refusals of *=, a pnpm field and a missing native-utils pin." }
  integration: { count: 131, covers: "Each bounded contract through the real thing. tests/replay.rs (W1) drives both doors over ephemeral ports against local HTTP, TLS and h2 origins that echo what reached them, so every wire claim is asserted on what the origin received: the edited header on the wire, a compose lands a flow, a missing field is a 400, an uncaptured seed body a 422 unless a body is given, TLS verified exactly as the proxy verifies (a rejected certificate is a 502 and lands nothing), an h2 upstream recorded as HTTP/2.0, a closed port answering with the reason and the ring's count unchanged, an evicted seed a 404, an over-cap reply recorded as not captured, the SSE request and response announcement, and the sent flow's view shape snapshot-locked. App.test.tsx (W3) drives the composer against both doors through the scripted client: the exact replay body for one edit, the row marked sent, the draft surviving another flow and reopened by replay, a new request gated on a URL and editable after its send, 422 and 502 refusals verbatim with the list unchanged, a late reply never speaking for the draft that replaced its own." }
  e2e: { count: 43, covers: "THE EPIC'S FULL FLOW, through the real Tauri window against a real ephemeral core and the harness's local TLS origin: cargo xtask ui e2e green, 4 of 4 spec files and 43 tests (onboarding 8, capture 3, replay 6, flows 26), with the run's tally judged so a run that passed fewer spec files than configured would be red. replay.spec.ts drives the plan's three journeys with no waiver. Replay with one edit: a proxied request to /echo opened, replay, the x-probe value changed and the only row marked edited, send disabled with sending... through the origin's two-second pause, pill, duration and open the new flow landing, the top row wearing the sent mark, and the new flow's response - the headers the origin received - carrying x-probe: edited, so the edit is asserted on the wire. Compose from scratch: send disabled with an empty URL, the echo URL sends and lands, the draft still takes typing afterwards. A send that fails: a closed local port gives the warning line with the core's connection-refused sentence, no pill and no duration, and the row count unchanged. The other three specs run in the same suite unchanged in what they assert; two of their selectors W3's markup had made stale were repaired at W4. TLS verification, h2 negotiation and the uncaptured-body refusal are asserted at integration, where they are exact, as the epic plan stated." }
